[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 475,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 432,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67788,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 432,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Haemangiomas are a family of benign vascular tumours.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 476,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 433,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67789,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 433,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9120/0 Haemangioma, NOS</p>\r\n<p>9121/0 Cavernous haemangioma</p>\r\n<p>9122/0 Venous haemangioma</p>\r\n<p>9132/0 Intramuscular haemangioma</p>\r\n<p>9123/0 Arteriovenous haemangioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 477,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 434,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67790,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 434,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F01 &amp; XH3U29 Benign neoplasm of intrathoracic organs &amp; Capillary haemangioma</p>\r\n<p>2F01 &amp; XH1GU2 Benign neoplasm of intrathoracic organs &amp; Cavernous haemangioma</p>\r\n<p>2F01 &amp; XH5AW4 Benign neoplasm of intrathoracic organs &amp; Haemangioma, NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 478,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 435,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67791,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 435,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> angioma; capillary haemangioma; cavernous haemangioma; venous malformation; arteriovenous malformation; venous or vascular ectasia; haemangiolymphangioma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 479,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 436,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67792,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 436,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 480,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 437,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67793,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 437,
        "headingText": "<p>Haemangiomas can occur at any thoracic site, including the thoracic vertebrae, chest wall, mediastinum (slightly more frequent in anterior [prevascular] than posterior [paravertebral]), thymus, lungs, and pleura {7705821; 15578892}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-01-04T13:03:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 481,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 438,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67794,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 438,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>As many as 50% of patients are asymptomatic. Others can present with nonspecific symptoms, including cough, chest pain, fever, or dyspnoea (due to compression/adhesion to adjacent structures) {7705821; 24347874}. Rare complications include dysphagia, superior vena cava syndrome, and neurological symptoms from intraspinal tumour extension {2198856; 10398787} and spontaneous haemothorax from rupture {26140771}. Haemangiomas can invade contiguous mediastinal/thoracic structures (e.g. the aorta, main pulmonary artery, venae cavae, phrenic/vagus nerves, and carotid artery), precluding complete excision {3592837}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 482,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 439,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67795,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 439,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Age ranges from congenital/newborn to the eighth decade of life.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 483,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 440,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67796,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 440,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Many lesions previously described as cavernous or venous haemangiomas are now considered to be venous malformations, as defined by the International Society for the Study of Vascular Anomalies classification {26055853}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 484,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 441,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67797,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 441,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 491,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 448,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67798,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 448,
        "headingText": "<p>These are typically circumscribed, cystic, haemorrhagic lesions ranging from 20 to 200 mm {7705821}. Appearances depend on type and anatomical site; lesions can be well or poorly defined or infiltrative with invasion of surrounding structures. They are typically reddish-purple and spongy or are firmer with thrombi/phleboliths.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-02-01T10:33:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 485,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 442,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67799,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 442,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This is a heterogeneous group, comprising proliferations of lymphatics, capillaries, or veins (with any predominating). Capillary haemangiomas are proliferations of small, thin-walled vessels with lobular and solid growth patterns, with dilated small vessels and more solid endothelial cell proliferations. Cavernous haemangiomas are relatively circumscribed proliferations of large, often dilated, thin-walled vessels or blood-filled sinuses lined by single endothelial layers. There may be fibrosis, interstitial inflammation, smooth muscle proliferation, and thrombosis {7705821; 3592837}, and sometimes regressive changes (e.g. hyalinization, dystrophic ossification, cystic and perivascular myxoid changes, and fatty overgrowth) {7705821}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 486,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 443,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67800,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 443,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 487,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 444,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67801,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 444,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 490,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 447,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67802,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 447,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Morphology dependent on type, with different types and calibres of essentially well-formed vessels lacking atypia</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Immunohistochemical evidence of endothelial differentiation</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 488,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 445,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67803,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 445,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 489,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 446,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67804,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 191,
        "headingId": 446,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Although these are benign neoplasms treated by surgical excision, they may behave aggressively, with destruction of neighbouring structures. Tumours that cannot be completely excised due to invasion of mediastinal structures have not shown evidence of progression, typically remaining stable without continued local invasion or aggressive recurrence {3592837}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:56:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]